Chapter: FsSpawnInherits
  cites Foreword chapter FileSystem

 The child faults inside `write-file` before it can report a verdict, and
 the os-backlog row names three candidate sites: the capability lookup, the
 servicer channel, and the elevation. One byte of verdict cannot tell them
 apart, so this arm is a TRAIL: the child writes a sentinel at each stage it
 reaches, and the parent reads how far the child got.

 THE STATES, and each names a different site:

    alive 0        the child never ran; nothing below means anything.
    write-cap 0    the FileSystem.Write bit did not travel to the child's
                   process-table word, so the lookup denies. The emitted
                   deny path RETURNS 0 rather than faulting, so this state
                   and a fault are different findings.
    read 0         the child faulted in the READ servicer, which shares the
                   lookup, the elevation and the call with the write path.
                   The site is in that common machinery.
    block-cap 0    the child does NOT hold `cap-block-device`, so a read that
                   reaches the disk reached it through the elevation cell and
                   nothing else. With this bit set the read arm would say
                   nothing about the elevation, which is why it is printed.
    read 72        the read servicer completed, so the lookup, the elevation
                   and the servicer call all work in a spawned process, and
                   the site is inside the write path alone.
    verdict 0      the child never reached the verdict, which is the fault.
    verdict 5      `write-file` returned False: a denial, not a fault.
    verdict 10     the write landed.

 The parent's own write is the control. The parent holds the same grant and
 runs in proc 0, where the servicer is known to work, so a parent write that
 fails means the disk or the fixture moved and no state below is evidence.

 The parent WAITS for the child rather than yielding once: a servicer
 round-trip is longer than one scheduler turn, and a peek that can race the
 poke reports the boot content of the cell instead of the verdict.

 The capability word is read at its address rather than through
 `read-process-grant`, because that function lives in a Verify chapter whose
 cite closure this arm deliberately does not carry (`fs-spawn-inherits.covers`).
 The three constants are the kernel's process table, fixed by the emitter:
 base 20480, 256-byte entries, the capability word at offset 56
 (`X86_64Boot.codex`, `proc-table-base` / `proc-entry-size` / `proc-cap-offset`),
 and `cap-filesystem-write` is bit 7 (`Capability.codex`).

Section: The Trail

  proc-table-base : Integer = 20480

  proc-entry-bytes : Integer = 256

  proc-cap-word-offset : Integer = 56

  cap-write-bit : Integer = 7

  cap-block-bit : Integer = 10

  my-cap-word : Integer -> Integer
  my-cap-word (pid) =
    peek-qword (proc-table-base + pid * proc-entry-bytes + proc-cap-word-offset) 0

  holds-bit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  holds-bit (pid) (bit) =
    if bit-and (bit-shru (my-cap-word pid) bit) 1 == 1 then 1 else 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Capability, Concurrent, Console, FileSystem.Read "OK", FileSystem.Write "OK"] Nothing
  opening = act
    parent-wrote <- write-file "OK.TXT" "parent data"
    let z0 = poke-byte 28000 0 0
    in let z1 = poke-byte 28000 1 0
    in let z2 = poke-byte 28000 2 0
    in let z3 = poke-byte 28000 3 0
    in let z4 = poke-byte 28000 4 0
    in act
      pid <- process-spawn (\x -> act
        let a = poke-byte 28000 1 71
        in let b = poke-byte 28000 2 (holds-bit process-get-pid cap-write-bit)
        in let d = poke-byte 28000 4 (holds-bit process-get-pid cap-block-bit)
        in act
          back <- read-text "OK.TXT"
          let c = poke-byte 28000 3 (if text-length back > 0 then 72 else 73)
          in poke-byte 28000 0 (if write-file "OK.TXT" "child data" then 10 else 5)
        end
      end)
      let w = process-wait pid
      in act
        print-line-uni ("fs-spawn-inherits parent-write " & (if parent-wrote then "1" else "0"))
        print-line-uni ("fs-spawn-inherits alive " & show (peek-byte 28000 1))
        print-line-uni ("fs-spawn-inherits write-cap " & show (peek-byte 28000 2))
        print-line-uni ("fs-spawn-inherits block-cap " & show (peek-byte 28000 4))
        print-line-uni ("fs-spawn-inherits read " & show (peek-byte 28000 3))
        print-line-uni ("fs-spawn-inherits verdict " & show (peek-byte 28000 0))
      end
    end
  end
